(* ============================================================================
   5. WHAT IS NOT IN THE SERIALIZED FORM. Formatter-internal nodes (PART 11,
      and the `raw_text` case profiles.md names as neither deniable nor
      carried) are not part of the document and are not serialized. Resolution results that a consumer can
      recompute - footnote numbering, caption numbers, a generated heading id -
      ARE serialized, because recomputing them requires reimplementing PART 9R.

      A GENERATED HEADING ID IS A RESOLUTION RESULT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-023]. A `heading`
      whose id was slugged from its text rather than written carries that id in
      `attrs.id`, the slot an authored `{#id}` occupies, and carries it in the
      resolved tree exactly as the two values above do.

      The criterion is PART 12 §5's own: recomputability. A heading id is NOT a function
      of the heading. Dedup assigns the next free suffix in document order, so
      two `# Notes` headings yield `Notes` and then `Notes-2` (CARVE-P2-004
      states the slug and dedup rules; CARVE-P9-019 shares that namespace with
      every other generated id). A consumer holding only the tree
      cannot replicate that, and a table of contents, a go-to-definition jump
      and a cross-document index each need the exact id, not a plausible one.

      CARVE-P12-005 IS NOT IN TENSION WITH THIS. CARVE-P12-005 forbids folding a resolution result
      in place of the construct the author wrote; here nothing authored is
      replaced. The id is published beside the heading's other attributes, the
      same way a resolved crossref keeps `key` and gains `href`.

   6. ROUND TRIP. `parse(x)` serialized and deserialized MUST equal `parse(x)`.
      A serializer that loses a field is not a lossy convenience; it is a
      consumer breaking silently one document later.

   7. THE DOCUMENT ROOT DECLARES ITS CONTRACT AND EXTENSIONS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-024]:
      A bare `document` MUST carry `type: "document"`, block `children`, and
      `astVersion: "2.0"`. It MAY carry `srcByteLength` when source bytes were
      measured; absence means unmeasured, while zero means an empty source.
      It MAY carry `extensions`, `stages`, `resources`, `metadata`, namespaced `data`,
      `attrs`, `dir`, `lang`, `pos`, and `segments` as the schema declares.
      No other root property is allowed. An extension declaration has one
      qualified `name`, a required boolean `required`, and an optional
      `version`; absent means the extension does not version its payload, and
      a producer MUST NOT declare a placeholder version in its place.
      Unknown required extensions cause refusal under CARVE-P12-056.
      An implementation MUST NOT hang document-level content off the root;
      frontmatter and definitions belong in the positioned tree.

      FRONTMATTER IS A BLOCK NODE, `type: "frontmatter"`, carrying:
        format   the info word on the opening fence (`frontmatter_format` in
                 PART 2), or `"yaml"` when the fence carries none
        value    the text between the fences, VERBATIM
      It is the FIRST child when present, since that is where it is written.

      CONTENT IS RAW, NOT PARSED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-025]. `value` is the source text.
      An implementation MUST NOT emit parsed key/value pairs in its place:
      parsing YAML or TOML is not the markup parser's job, the raw text plus
      `format` lets any consumer do it, and a parsed map cannot represent a
      document whose frontmatter is malformed. A parsed map held internally is
      an internal choice, and PART 12 §1 already requires mapping on the way out rather
      than exporting internals.

      A FOOTNOTE DEFINITION IS A BLOCK NODE, `type: "footnote"`, carrying:
        key       the label as written, without the `[^` and `]:`
        children  the definition body, parsed as blocks
      `key` identifies the footnote reference; `attrs.id` remains an authored
      document anchor and has a different purpose.

      A DEFINITION IS A CHILD OF THE DOCUMENT even when it was authored inside
      a container. PART 9 §16 already makes a definition document-level
      metadata that is collected out of its blockquote or list item, leaving
      that container empty; the serialized tree follows the same rule rather
      than inventing a second one. Its `pos` still records where it was
      WRITTEN, so nothing about navigation is lost by the move.

      AN ABBREVIATION DEFINITION IS A BLOCK NODE, `type: "abbreviation_def"`,
      carrying:
        key        the term as written, without the `*[` and `]:`
        expansion  the expansion text, without the separating space
      It renders nothing itself; it is what the inline `abbreviation` resolves
      against.

      THE HOISTING CLAUSE ABOVE IS ABOUT FOOTNOTES. An `abbreviation_def` is
      never hoisted, because there is never one to hoist: the construct is
      recognized only at document level.

      AN ABBREVIATION DEFINITION IS RECOGNIZED ONLY AT DOCUMENT LEVEL --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-026]. `*[TERM]: expansion` is an `abbreviation_definition` only as a
      direct child of the document. Written inside a block quote, a list item
      or a div, the line is not a definition at all: it is ordinary paragraph
      text, it defines nothing, and it is preserved as the text the author
      typed.

          > *[HTML]: Hyper Text

          The HTML spec.

      renders a block quote holding the literal text `*[HTML]: Hyper Text`,
      and `The HTML spec.` with no `<abbr>`.

      This is the same fallback an unrecognized definition marker already
      takes: `*[HTML]:<TAB>x` is a paragraph because the separator must be a
      literal space (see the note at `abbreviation_definition`), and the marker
      line "is preserved as text" there too. The rule is reused, not invented.

      Unlike footnotes, abbreviations have no use-site marker and rewrite every
      occurrence of the term. They are therefore recognized only at document
      level, leaving quoted or foreign container content inert.

      THE FORMATTER CONSEQUENCE IS ACCEPTED, NOT OVERLOOKED. Hoisting means
      PART 11's writer emits the definition after the container the author
      wrote it in, which reads like a violation of PART 11 §6 ("fmt does not
      reorder ... those are the author's choices and the AST records them"). It
      is not, because PART 11 §6 governs what the writer may do with what the tree
      HOLDS, and this clause is what the tree holds. It is accepted for
      footnotes because a footnote definition renders as a footnote either way,
      so the move costs the author placement and nothing else.

      Definitions appear in DOCUMENT ORDER by source position. Numbering is a
      resolution result and stays on the reference (PART 12 §5).

   8. A DEFINITION LIST'S ENTRIES ARE NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-027]. `definition_list`
      carries `children`: the `<dt>` / `<dd>` sequence in DOCUMENT ORDER, as
      `definition_term` and `definition_description` nodes.

        {"type":"definition_list","children":[
          {"type":"definition_term","children":[...]},
          {"type":"definition_term","children":[...]},
          {"type":"definition_description","children":[...]}]}

      A `definition_term` carries INLINE children, a `definition_description`
      BLOCK children, and either may carry `attrs` and `pos` like any other
      node. A run of terms belongs to the descriptions that follow it, which is
      the rule the rendered list already shows -- so the grouping is derivable
      and is not published.

      NOT publishing it is the decision. `definition_term` and
      `definition_description` are in the profiles.md block vocabulary, so a
      profile can NAME them; under an object form those two entries named
      nothing, and a host writing `denyBlock(['definition_term'])` got silence,
      which profiles.md calls the specific failure a normative vocabulary
      exists to prevent.

      `definition_list.spacing` is `tight` or `loose`, as on an ordinary
      list. A source writer chooses its spacing from the authored blank-line
      and wrapper facts. The 1.2 `loose` boolean is not serialized. This one
      field gives both list kinds the same polarity and lets an importer state
      the desired wrapper behavior without a derived default.

      A blank line between entries does not by itself wrap a `<dd>` in a
      paragraph; a second block inside a description does. The spacing field
      records the choice an importer cannot recover from entry boundaries.
      `tight` and `loose` now share one polarity with ordinary lists, while
      the typed term and description sequence keeps profile policy visible.

   9. HOW DEEP AN INGESTED AST MAY NEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-028]. Reading a serialized AST
      is a recursive descent over attacker-controlled structure, so it needs a
      bound for the same reason the parser does (CARVE-P9-054, RESOURCE
      BOUNDS).

      (a) An implementation MUST ACCEPT any AST ITS OWN PARSER CAN PRODUCE at
          MAX_NESTING_DEPTH: `parse` piped into ingest MUST NOT fail on a
          document the same build just parsed.

      (b) It MUST REJECT deeper input with an ingest refusal (CARVE-P12-068)
          naming the depth bound.

      (c) The bound MAY be counted in WHATEVER UNIT suits the implementation
          (JSON structural levels, AST node levels, recursion frames). An
          implementation MUST NOT publish its unit as a shared contract.

      The conversion from MAX_NESTING_DEPTH is NOT A CONSTANT: one AST level
      costs two JSON structural levels for a blockquote or div and four for a
      list (list, items, item, children). An implementation MUST DERIVE its
      bound from MAX_NESTING_DEPTH by the worst per-level cost of its own
      encoding, and MUST NOT restate the parser's number as its ingest number.

   10. A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION IS A NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-029].
      `type: "link_reference_definition"`, carrying:
        key     the reference label as the author wrote it, unnormalized
        href    the destination; present, including the empty string for a
                bare `[label]:` definition (CARVE-P3-024)
        title   the quoted title, unescaped, when the definition carries one
        attrs   a trailing attribute block on the definition line (CARVE-P3-007)

      IT HOISTS TO THE DOCUMENT, exactly as CARVE-P12-024 requires of the FOOTNOTE
      definition: a definition authored inside a block quote or a list item is
      a child of the DOCUMENT, and its `pos` still says where it was written.
      The container is left as the author's remaining content, which is what
      corpus 117-footnote-definition-inside-a-container-is-collected already
      pins for the footnote case.

      THE ABBREVIATION DEFINITION IS NOT THE THIRD KIND HERE. CARVE-P12-024's clause AN
      ABBREVIATION DEFINITION IS RECOGNIZED ONLY AT DOCUMENT LEVEL says the
      opposite for that one: inside a container the line is not a definition
      at all, it is paragraph text, and nothing is hoisted because nothing was
      collected.

      A node carries the definition's required `pos`, attributes and document
      order, so document order rather than merge order decides which definition
      wins; a root map cannot represent those properties directly.

      A DEFINITION IS STILL INVISIBLE IN HTML. The node renders nothing on that
      target; it feeds the links and images that resolve its label (PART 9R R1).
      The non-HTML targets emit it, as CARVE-P11-028 requires.

   11. A PROPERTY THE SCHEMA DOES NOT NAME IS REJECTED ON INGEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-030].
      `resources/ast-schema.json` closes every node with
      `additionalProperties: false`, so a payload carrying a property no node
      type names is NOT a Carve AST. An ingest MUST REFUSE it (CARVE-P12-068),
      naming the property and the PATH it appeared at. It neither drops the
      property nor passes it through: a reader that copies the record re-emits
      the property, and its own output no longer validates.

      The declared `data` field is the sole must-ignore area. Its keys are
      globally qualified extension names declared on the root or envelope.
      A reader preserves unknown optional `data` without interpreting it.
      It refuses an unknown required extension before reading the tree.
      Core properties remain closed in every minor version (CARVE-P12-056).

   12. A ROOT THAT DEVIATES FROM §7 IS REJECTED ON INGEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-031]. CARVE-P12-024
      requires `type`, `children`, and `astVersion`; the schema pins their
      types and pins `type` to `document`. `srcByteLength` is optional and
      means measured source bytes only when present.

      AN INGEST REFUSAL IS A TYPED ERROR NAMING WHAT AND WHERE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-068]. Where PART 12 says an ingest refuses, it fails with an
      error of its own: typed, documented, and naming what was wrong and, for
      a node, the path it appeared at. Not truncation, not a crash, not a
      silent drop or repair, and not whatever a JSON library happened to raise.
      An ingest that accepts a tree and then handles only part of it has told
      the caller nothing.

      An ingest MUST REFUSE:

      (a) A ROOT MISSING ANY OF THE THREE REQUIRED FIELDS. Not defaulted, not inferred:
          a reader that supplies `children: []` has turned a truncated document
          into an empty one.
      (b) A ROOT CARRYING ANYTHING THE SCHEMA DOES NOT NAME. The root is closed like every other
          node (CARVE-P12-030). A root whose `type` is not `document` (`doc`, say, which
          is ProseMirror's) fails (d).
      (c) A NODE WHOSE `type` THE SCHEMA DOES NOT NAME, AT DECODE, not in a
          renderer one step later: a formatter, a linter or a language server
          may never reach a renderer.
      (d) A PAYLOAD THAT DOES NOT VALIDATE AGAINST `resources/ast-schema.json`,
          at decode. The schema is the list of what is checked; (a) to (c) and
          CARVE-P12-030 keep their own statements.

      (c) IS ABOUT NODE POSITIONS: the root, and every field the schema fills
      with a node. Which fields those are depends on the type that carries them.
      `children` is primary content; secondary node slots are read from the
      schema. The check never reaches
      inside `attrs.keyValues`, whose values are strings: `[x](/u){type=widget}`
      puts `{"type":"widget"}` there, and refusing it would refuse a document
      the engine's own parser produced.

      A `srcByteLength` THAT IS PRESENT BUT WRONG is not refused. Nothing in the
      tree depends on it, so readers ignore its value; (a) is about presence,
      and the schema constrains only its type and sign.

   13. SOURCE LAYOUT IS A SEPARATE OPT-IN SIDECAR -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-032]. A producer MAY
      return source-fidelity metadata beside an AST, as a sidecar under
      CARVE-P12-067.

      A SIDECAR TRAVELS BESIDE THE TREE AND NEVER IN IT -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-067]. The source-layout (CARVE-P12-032), node-identity (CARVE-P12-060),
      annotation-range (CARVE-P12-062) and provenance (CARVE-P12-063) sidecars share these rules:

      - A producer returns one only when the caller asks for it, and MUST NOT
        put its data in the tree: PART 12 §2, CARVE-P12-024, CARVE-P12-030 and CARVE-P12-031 keep the tree closed, and
        two documents differing only in sidecar content are the same document
        under PART 12 §6.
      - It carries `version` 1 and validates against its own schema. A reader
        refuses a version it does not implement, and an AST decoder does not
        accept a sidecar as an AST. Default parse, AST encoding, CLI JSON and
        rendering do not move.
      - A `path` is an RFC 6901 pointer into the accompanying canonical AST.
      - A byte range counts the bytes of the ORIGINAL input it names, BOM and
        CRLF bytes included: `startByte` inclusive, `endByte` exclusive. It is
        not a PART 12 §4 offset, which counts UTF-8 bytes.
      - SIDECAR DATA DOES NOT SURVIVE A CANONICAL WRITE: Carve source has no
        spelling for it, and the write produces a new input.

      The source-layout sidecar validates against
      `resources/ast-source-layout-schema.json`. Its encoding is UTF-8,
      `source` is the original input, `lineEndings` names the terminators it
      uses (`none`, `lf`, `crlf`, `cr` or `mixed`), and `bom` says whether it
      begins with U+FEFF. Entries are sorted by path; a producer MUST NOT
      invent an unplaceable range.

      Original source and byte ranges are the portable minimum. Every OTHER
      property the schema declares is an optional MEASURED FACT, defined
      below. ABSENT MEANS UNKNOWN, per field: a producer that did
      not measure a fact omits it, a producer MUST NOT emit a fact it did not
      measure, and a consumer MUST NOT read an omission as a default value.

      F1 `markerRaw` -- the exact source text of the marker that opened the
         node, WITHOUT the separator that follows it (PART 9 §24 C2). `-`,
         `*`, `1.`, `1)`, `iv.` for a list item; the `::` of a definition
         term.
      F2 `markerColumn` -- the visual column the marker's first character
         sits at, in PART 9 §24 C1's arithmetic and therefore ZERO-BASED: a
         marker at the left margin is 0, and a marker after one leading tab is
         4. It is NOT `pos.startColumn`, which is 1-based and counts
         UTF-8 bytes; the two may disagree on non-ASCII text.
      F3 `contentColumn` -- content_column(node) exactly as PART 9 §24 C3
         defines it, in the same zero-based arithmetic as F2, so `- ` is 2 and
         `10. ` is 4.
      F4 `continuationStyle` -- how the node's later lines reached it. One
         value, chosen by PRECEDENCE, because a node may be written in more
         than one of these ways at once: `explicit` if any line was reached
         through the `+` continuation marker (PART 9 §17 L3/L4), else `lazy`
         if any paragraph line sits BELOW the content column, else `indent` if
         any later line REACHES the content column -- at it or past it, since
         PART 9 §24 C3 asks a child to reach the column, not to land on it --
         else `marker_line` if all
         content is on the marker line, else `none` where the node takes no
         continuation. The order is the order a writer must respect: the
         spellings that cannot be re-derived from the tree win over the ones
         that can.
      F5 `blankLinesBefore`, `blankLinesAfter` -- how many lines holding
         nothing but PART 2 `whitespace` sit immediately before and after the
         node's range within its own container. The count is the AUTHORED one,
         which is what makes it a fact rather than a re-derivation: the parse
         reads a longer run and a single blank the same way in most positions.
      F6 `openerRaw`, `closerRaw` -- the exact source text of the delimiter
         run that opened and closed a fenced node, its info string excluded.
         ABSENCE STILL MEANS UNKNOWN here, so an unterminated block is not
         spelled by omitting `closerRaw`: it is `closerRaw` present and EMPTY.
         A producer that measures fences at all emits both, and `openerRaw` is
         never empty because a fence cannot open without a delimiter.
      F7 `metadataSeparatorRaw` -- the exact separator run between a label or
         term and its body, its whitespace included, for the definition-style
         openers that take one.
      F8 `leadingPipe`, `trailingPipe` -- whether the row was AUTHORED with
         the optional leading and trailing `|`.
      F9 `attachment` -- how the node reached the host it belongs to.
         `preceding` for the host immediately above it (a caption slot under
         its host, PART 9 §4), `continuation` for a `+`-attached block,
         `detached` where an attachable position was written but the spelling
         detached it (PART 9 §4's two blank lines), `none` where the node has no
         host.

      A DECLARED FACT WITH NO DEFINITION IS A DEFECT, not a reserved slot.

   14. A CAPTION IS A NODE WITH BLOCK CONTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-033].
      A `figure` or `table` carries a `caption` node when it has a visible
      caption. Its `children` are blocks; `short`, when present, is inline
      navigation text. The node may carry `attrs`, `pos`, `segments`, `data`,
      `metadata`, `dir`, and `lang`. An absent `short` means none was supplied.
      The fallback matrix row `caption.short` records target behavior.
      A processor MUST NOT derive it by truncating the full caption.
      It MUST NOT derive one by taking the first sentence either: abbreviation
      is an editorial choice and may depend on language. A canonical writer
      MUST omit `caption.short` and MUST NOT substitute it for the full caption.

      A table inside a figure keeps its own caption. The outer figure caption
      describes the figure as a whole and does not replace the table caption.
      Ordinary HTML, plain, and ANSI output render the visible caption once;
      a structured exporter may use `short` for navigation. The canonical
      writer reports loss when it cannot spell a block caption or `short`.

      A SHAPE NO CARVE SOURCE SPELLS IS CARRIED, NOT INVENTED -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-066]. An interchange-only shape is a node, field, or wrapper
      supplied by an AST consumer or format bridge. The canonical writer MUST
      NOT invent source syntax for it. The conversion diagnostics report any
      information it cannot write, including block captions, composite figure
      structure, block cell content, small caps, math numbering, sections,
      ruby, and extension payloads. A writer that cannot preserve opaque
      `data` reports that loss as well (CARVE-P12-073).
      Source parsers do not synthesize an interchange-only shape. AST
      encode/decode preserves one under PART 12 §6; its own clause defines
      target rendering and the corresponding loss report.

   15. TABLE ROW GROUPS ARE NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-034].
      A `table` carries `children` consisting of `table_head`, `table_body`,
      and `table_foot` nodes in document order. Each group carries `children`
      of `table_row` nodes. A body may carry `headRows` and `rowHeadColumns`
      for its own intermediate header rows and leading row-header columns.
      An absent value means zero. A body whose split the source declared
      (`header-rows`, `body-rows` and the other partition attributes) MUST
      carry `headRows`, `0` included; a body the producer inferred omits it,
      so a consumer can tell an authored partition from an inferred one. A
      table may have no head or foot, and may
      have multiple bodies. Its groups cover every row exactly once by
      construction; `rowGroups` counts and a flat `rows` array are not 2.0.
      An authored empty head or foot with attributes remains an empty group,
      since dropping it would lose those attributes.

      `table_body.headRows` states the boundary between its intermediate
      header rows and data rows. A leading run of header cells cannot recover
      that boundary when the first body also begins with headers. A body's
      `rowHeadColumns` counts leading row-header columns in that body's data
      rows; an individual cell's authored `header` flag remains independent.
      This is why row-header metadata belongs on the body rather than on the
      whole table ([carve#2402](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2402)).

      A source pipe table with no explicit grouping has a head containing the
      leading consecutive all-header rows and one body for the remaining rows.
      A later all-header row belongs to the body unless a format bridge
      supplies a separate group. A `table_row` carries cell `children`; a
      filter that removes a row also removes it from its group. A consumer
      MUST NOT infer a new group from header flags after ingest.

      A pipe table's preceding block-attribute line MAY carry
      `header-rows=N` and `footer-rows=N`. An empty boolean value means one;
      otherwise N MUST be a non-negative decimal integer. The two counts
      MUST NOT exceed the number of source rows. The parser makes a
      `table_head` and `table_foot` for the stated ranges and one body for
      rows between them when any remain. An explicit header row promotes its
      cells to column headers in HTML, but does not change an authored `|>`
      marker or column default. A native `|=>` still sets its starting
      column's default in that row.

      A pipe table MAY additionally carry `body-rows="N,..."`. Each entry
      gives one body's data-row count; an empty value means no bodies and a
      zero entry means one empty body. With valid `body-rows`, an omitted
      `header-rows` means zero leading head rows. `body-header-rows="N,..."`
      counts intermediate header rows per body and defaults to zero when
      omitted. `body-header-cols="N,..."` counts leading row-header columns;
      an empty entry leaves that body's `rowHeadColumns` absent while zero
      explicitly records zero. Both positional lists require `body-rows` and
      MUST have exactly the same number of entries as bodies. An empty
      header-row entry is invalid. An empty header-column entry occupies one
      position; with zero bodies both header lists MUST be absent. Counts
      MUST be non-negative decimal integers no greater than 9007199254740991.
      The head, body headers and data rows, and foot MUST account for every
      source row exactly once. Invalid body metadata synthesizes no explicit
      grouping; all row-group metadata stays ordinary attributes in HTML.
      Valid metadata is consumed there, without changing cells' authored
      `header` fields.

      The ListTable extension also authors header-led bodies through its
      `header-row` markers. Its list node shape is distinct from a core table.

      The canonical writer and renderers use the group order and attributes.
      If Carve source cannot spell a group's boundaries or row-header count,
      the writer reports `field-unspellable` for `table_head`, `table_foot`,
      `table_body.headRows`, or `table_body.rowHeadColumns` through the
      CARVE-P11-046 channel. The removed `rowGroups.headAttrs` and
      `rowGroups.footAttrs` are mapped to group `attrs` before conversion.

      HTML renders a head, each body, and a foot in order. An empty head or
      foot remains visible as a group when it carries attributes, and every
      body renders a `tbody`, including an empty body. Section attributes use
      ordinary escaping and safe-mode filtering. Row spans stop at section
      boundaries when section attributes are present; a continuation at the
      start of a section becomes an empty cell. Plain and ANSI output retain
      row order. A canonical writer supplies missing source metadata for an
      explicit grouping, including a single empty body. It MUST NOT replace
      conflicting authored attributes or drop/reorder rows. If retained
      attributes cannot express the groups, it reports `field-unspellable`
      through CARVE-P11-046.

   16. A COMPOSITE FIGURE IS ITS OWN NODE TYPE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-035]
      (CARVE-P9-001). The type is `figure_group`, and the wire shape:

        {"type":"figure_group","children":[...],
         "caption":{"type":"caption","children":[...]},"attrs":{...},"pos":{...}}

      `children` are ordinary block nodes IN SOURCE ORDER: the panels are the
      `figure` and `table` nodes among them, exactly the nodes the inner
      caption rules built, and non-panel stray content sits between them in
      place. There is NO `panels` array: repeating the children under a
      second key would let the two disagree, the same contradiction CARVE-P12-034's
      counts-not-copies rule exists to make unrepresentable, and a consumer
      derives the panel list the way the renderer does -- by type, in order.

      `caption` is the GROUP caption as a positioned caption node; absent means
      the group is uncaptioned (no empty placeholder). `type` and
      `children` are required, `attrs` optional, `pos` per PART 12 §4.

      DISCRIMINATED BY TYPE, NOT BY SHAPE. Both `figure` and `figure_group`
      carry block `children`. The type string tells a consumer whether those
      blocks are one figure's content or several captionable panels.

      NO legends or separate label fields. The group's caption may carry
      `short` for navigation under CARVE-P12-033. The schema
      (`resources/ast-schema.json`) pins the shape; an unknown property on a
      `figure_group` is rejected on ingest like any other (CARVE-P12-030).
   ============================================================================ *)
